# Template for the opt-in E2E suites. Copy to `.env.e2e` and fill in.
#
#     cp .env.e2e.example .env.e2e && npm run test:e2e
#
# `.env.e2e` is git-ignored because it holds credentials for real third-party
# accounts. This template is committed and must never carry a real value.
#
# Every suite skips cleanly when its variables are missing, so you only need to
# fill in the backends you actually want to exercise. A variable already set in
# the environment overrides the file, which keeps one-off runs possible:
#
#     E2E_MDBLIST_API_KEY=other-key npm run test:e2e

# --- Floppy ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Self-hosted, so the safest of the three: point it at a throwaway instance.
# The token comes from Settings -> Advanced of a dedicated account.
E2E_FLOPPY_URL=http://localhost:8000
E2E_FLOPPY_API_KEY=

# --- mdblist --------------------------------------------------------------
# A REAL hosted account. The free tier allows 4 static lists and 1000 requests
# per day; the suite creates one private list and deletes it afterwards.
E2E_MDBLIST_API_KEY=

# --- Trakt ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Consumes an ALREADY-OBTAINED token and never runs the OAuth device flow: run
# the application once to authorise, then point E2E_TRAKT_SAVE_FILE at the token
# file it wrote. A free account is capped at 5 personal lists; the suite creates
# one, deletes it afterwards, and reclaims any list an interrupted run left.
E2E_TRAKT_CLIENT_ID=
E2E_TRAKT_CLIENT_SECRET=
E2E_TRAKT_SAVE_FILE=./config/.trakt

# --- FlixPatrol XPath drift canary ----------------------------------------
# Hits live FlixPatrol pages, which sit behind Cloudflare, so a local
# FlareSolverr is required:
#
#     docker run -d --name flaresolverr -p 8191:8191 ghcr.io/flaresolverr/flaresolverr:latest
E2E_FLARESOLVERR_URL=http://localhost:8191/v1
